      title: "5 · Nobody writes it down: the knowledge of the road",
      section: "Section 2 · The road",
      body: `Getting the job is one problem. Doing a job that happens in a different building every week is a completely different one, and almost nobody teaches it.

**Hannah Storm's route.** Notre Dame, class of 1983, in communications and political science (University of Notre Dame, n.d.). First woman to host *CNN Sports Tonight*, in 1989. At NBC from the early 1990s she became the first woman in American television to solo host a broadcast network's national sports series, covering Major League Baseball including three World Series, plus the NBA on NBC and four Olympics. The load-bearing word in that claim is *solo*: Andrea Joyce preceded her as a co-host, so the wider version of the sentence is false and the narrower one is what ESPN and Storm's own site use. On June 21, 1997, she called the WNBA's first game, on play-by-play rather than from a studio. She moved to CBS, then to ESPN in 2008, and later called an entire NFL season for Amazon's *Thursday Night Football* alongside Andrea Kremer.

**What accumulates over a career like that.** Not just tape. Venue knowledge: where the truck parks, which credential gets you onto the field and which one stops at the tunnel, how long load-in takes at a building with one freight elevator, which press box has no sight line to the far corner, who at each venue actually answers the phone. This is real professional capital and it is almost never written down. It lives in notebooks and in conversations, and when the person leaves the industry it leaves with them.

**Why that is a defect and not a charming tradition.** Knowledge that exists only in one head cannot be sold, taught, delegated, or inherited. It also cannot be used as evidence when you are negotiating a rate, because you cannot show it. An itinerant contractor who writes the road down converts private experience into a transferable asset.

**What writing it down looks like.** A per-venue file with access notes, contact names, timings, parking and load-in, and the specific thing that went wrong last time. A per-client file with rate history and what was included. A running log of miles and expenses, kept during the trip rather than reconstructed in April. None of this is glamorous, and all of it survives you.

:::reveal Why is the word "solo" load-bearing in Hannah Storm's network-sports-series first? ||| Andrea Joyce hosted a network sports series before her as a co-host. Storm's documented claim is the first woman in American television to SOLO host one, and dropping "solo" makes the sentence false.

:::reveal What is road knowledge, and why is a career's worth of it usually worth nothing to anyone else? ||| Venue-specific operating knowledge: access, credentials, load-in timing, sight lines, who answers the phone. It is worth nothing to anyone else because it is almost never written down, so it cannot be taught, sold, or used as evidence in a rate negotiation.

## Vocabulary
- **Play-by-play**: the broadcaster who describes the action as it happens, distinct from the analyst who explains it.
- **Load-in**: the process of getting crew and equipment into a venue and set up before a broadcast.
- **Credential**: the pass that defines exactly which parts of a venue a worker may enter, and when.
- **Road knowledge**: venue-specific and client-specific operating detail accumulated by working, which has value only once it is recorded.

## Sources
ESPN Press Room. (n.d.). *Hannah Storm*. https://espnpressroom.com/us/bios/storm_hannah/

University of Notre Dame, Department of Film, Television, and Theatre. (n.d.). *Hannah Storm*. https://ftt.nd.edu/careers/alumni-network/hannah-storm/`,
