      slug: "sts-career-as-portfolio",
      title: "7 · A career is a portfolio, not a job",
      section: "Section 2 · The road",
      body: `The single most useful reframe in this course is that a broadcast career is not one job held for a long time. It is hundreds of separate assignments across decades and several employers, and the people who last manage it the way an investor manages holdings.

**Robin Roberts's route.** Southeastern Louisiana University, cum laude in 1983, and one of only three women in that school's history with 1,000 career points and 1,000 rebounds. Local sports radio and television in Mississippi, Tennessee and Georgia. ESPN from 1990 to 2005, anchoring *SportsCenter* and becoming the first woman to co-host *NFL PrimeTime*. Her employer describes her as the first Black woman sports journalist at ESPN, and because that claim comes from the company she worked for it should be attributed rather than stated flat. She moved to *Good Morning America* as a full-time co-anchor in 2005 and was still co-anchoring it decades later. Along the way: a Peabody Award in 2012, the Women's Basketball Hall of Fame in 2012, the Arthur Ashe Courage Award in 2013, and Disney Legend in 2019.

**What a portfolio view actually changes.** If your career is a job, a bad year is a crisis and a lost client is a catastrophe. If it is a portfolio, a bad year is a drawdown in one holding, and the question becomes concentration: how much of this year's income came from one client, and what happens if that client changes management. Every contractor in this course has faced that question and the ones who kept working had an answer before they needed it.

**Three portfolio questions worth asking annually.** What share of my income came from my largest client? Which of my assignments built a skill I can sell somewhere else, and which only paid? Which relationship is with a person rather than with an organization, and what happens when that person moves?

**Where the analogy stops.** Roberts worked through breast cancer in 2007 and myelodysplastic syndrome in 2012 in public. That is a fact about her life and not a professional technique, and a course that turns it into a lesson about resilience has stopped teaching and started flattering. This course does not do that.

:::reveal What question does a portfolio view of a career make you ask that a job view does not? ||| Concentration: what share of income comes from a single client or employer, and what happens to the rest of the portfolio if that one holding fails.

:::reveal Why does this course attribute the "first Black woman sports journalist at ESPN" claim rather than assert it? ||| It comes from her own employer's publications. Employer-sourced firsts are usable but should be presented as the company's claim, because no independent record was found confirming it.

## Vocabulary
- **Portfolio view**: treating a working life as a set of separate, individually manageable assignments rather than one continuous job.
- **Concentration risk**: the exposure created when a large share of income depends on one client or employer.
- **Drawdown**: a decline in one holding that the rest of a portfolio is expected to absorb.
- **Attributed claim**: a statement presented as "according to X" because the only source for it is an interested party.

## Sources
ABC News. (n.d.). *Robin Roberts biography*. https://abcnews.com/GMA/robin-roberts-biography/story?id=128237

Sports Broadcasting Hall of Fame. (n.d.). *Robin Roberts*. https://www.sportsbroadcastinghalloffame.org/inductees/robin-roberts/`,
