      title: "11 · Presenter, anchor, commentator: the crafts are not the same",
      section: "Section 3 · The job has no border",
      body: `This lesson does two jobs at once. It introduces a broadcaster whose English-language record is unusually thin, and it uses that thinness to teach how to write about someone when the sources are weak.

**Carol Tshabalala's documented record.** Born in 1981 in Pimville, Soweto. She started at about eighteen as an apprentice on the South African Broadcasting Corporation's *Sports Buzz*, worked in SABC television and radio including Kaya FM and Radio 2000 through 2011, and joined SuperSport International in 2011, where she hosts *Extra Time*. Since 2016 she has also worked as a live studio anchor for Premier League Productions in the United Kingdom. South African media call her the First Lady of Sport (SuperSport, n.d.; News24, 2024).

**The distinction that keeps getting erased.** She is a presenter and studio anchor. That is a different craft from commentary. A commentator calls live action; a presenter runs the program around it, setting up segments, interviewing, managing panelists, and holding the broadcast together in real time when a feed drops. Calling a presenter a commentator is the same class of error as calling a line judge a referee: it swaps a real job for a more familiar one and gets the person's actual skill wrong.

**What this course will not say about her.** The source scripts claimed she commentated at the 2010 FIFA World Cup. The research pass found no support for it, and in 2010 she was still at the SABC. Her documented connection to that tournament is the Final Draw in Cape Town in December 2009, where the on-stage hosts were other people. So the claim is not repeated here, in either a softened or a hedged form.

**Why the sourcing is thin, and what that means.** English-language coverage of her is dominated by speaker-bureau biographies and celebrity aggregator sites that copy each other word for word. That is a structure in which a single unverified line becomes hundreds of apparently independent confirmations. The correct response is to restrict claims to what the broadcaster she works for will support, and to say out loud that the rest is unestablished.

**The idea that survives all of this.** Sports broadcasting is one global trade. The freelancer's problems, rates, contracts, venues, currencies, and time zones, are structurally the same in Johannesburg as in Indianapolis, and a course written only for the American market is quietly parochial.

:::reveal What is the difference between a presenter and a commentator, and why does the swap matter? ||| A commentator calls live action; a presenter runs the program around it, handling segments, interviews, panelists and live problems. Swapping them misdescribes the person's actual craft, the same way calling a line judge a referee does.

:::reveal Why do many apparently independent sources about Carol Tshabalala not count as corroboration? ||| Most of them are speaker-bureau biographies and aggregator pages that copy one another verbatim, so one unverified line reappears many times without ever being independently checked.

## Vocabulary
- **Presenter**: the broadcaster who fronts and runs a program, called an anchor or host in American usage.
- **Studio anchor**: a presenter working from a studio rather than from the venue, often across several live feeds.
- **Speaker-bureau biography**: promotional copy supplied by the subject or an agency, which is not independent evidence.
- **Circular sourcing**: many pages appearing to confirm a claim while all of them copy a single unverified original.

## Sources
News24. (2024, April 12). *23 years of sportscaster Carol Tshabalala*. https://www.news24.com/truelove/celebrity/23-years-of-sportscaster-carol-tshabalala-20240412

SuperSport. (n.d.). *Carol Tshabalala's passion made her a well of sporting knowledge*. https://supersport.com/football/news/ea7a550e-ab19-4e93-89c1-22b9228ea200/carol-tshabalala-s-passion-made-her-a-well-of-sporting-knowledge`,
