      title: "14 · The pipeline program, and what happens after it",
      section: "Section 4 · The ladder nobody shows you",
      body: `The last section is the one that makes the course honest, because two of its three subjects are no longer with the organization that hired them.

**Maia Chaka's route.** A Norfolk State University graduate in 2006. She taught health and physical education at a Virginia Beach school for roughly fifteen years while officiating football on the side: high school, then Pac-12 regular-season games from 2018, then the XFL. She entered the NFL's Officiating Development Program in 2014. In March 2021 the NFL hired her, and on September 12, 2021, she worked Jets against Panthers, becoming the first Black woman to officiate an NFL game (NFL, 2021).

**She was a line judge, not a referee.** In American football the referee is one specific position, the crew chief in the white hat. Chaka never held it, and the NFL's own release calls her an official. Almost every headline about her says referee. This is the role-inflation failure from lesson 3, happening to a real person in real time.

**How the ladder actually works.** High school, then college conference, then a developmental league, then a league-run pipeline program, then hiring. In her case that pipeline ran seven years before the hire. A course that presents entry as a decision somebody makes about you, rather than as a ladder with named rungs and a known duration, is not giving anyone anything to do.

**And then what happened.** She worked three NFL seasons, 2021 through 2023, and did not return for 2024. Officiating trade coverage reported no public statement about whether the separation was voluntary (Football Zebras, 2024). This course does not speculate, because nobody outside the situation knows. It states the fact, because the fact is the most important thing in the story: getting in is a rung, not a destination, and a curriculum that ends at the hiring press release has taught students to expect something that frequently does not happen.

:::reveal Why does this course call Maia Chaka an official or line judge rather than a referee? ||| Referee is one specific on-field position, the crew chief, and she never held it. The NFL's own announcement calls her an official; the headlines that say referee are inflating the role.

:::reveal What are the named rungs of the officiating ladder she climbed, and roughly how long did the last one take? ||| High school, then a college conference (Pac-12 from 2018), then a developmental league (XFL), then the NFL's Officiating Development Program, which she entered in 2014 and which ran about seven years before her 2021 hire.

## Vocabulary
- **Line judge**: an on-field NFL official responsible for the line of scrimmage and related calls, distinct from the referee.
- **Referee**: the crew chief of an officiating crew, the position that announces penalties, one role among seven on an NFL crew.
- **Pipeline program**: an employer-run development track built to move candidates from outside a profession into it.
- **Rung**: one named, achievable stage of an entry ladder, as opposed to an unexplained leap.

## Sources
Football Zebras. (2024, April). *Trailblazing official Maia Chaka is out of the NFL*. https://www.footballzebras.com/2024/04/trailblazing-official-maia-chaka-is-out-of-the-nfl/

NFL. (2021). *Maia Chaka first Black woman named to NFL officiating staff*. https://www.nfl.com/_amp/maia-chaka-first-black-woman-named-nfl-officiating-staff`,
